#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "b71e74ab-9cb3-5e43-94aa-52c2a926dc5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21874" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+2y=f(x), \\qquad y'(0)=0,\\quad y'(1)=-5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p'(0) = 0$ and $y_p'(1) = 0.$*\n![](images/50/50bc50685896c736abcf63fd30cee09ac314c98714ed91251e568d714611c5ec.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y=y_c+y_p$, where $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation $y''+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+y_p'(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since $y_p'(0)=0$ and $y_p'(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny'(0)=y_c'(0)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(1)=y_c'(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y'(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y'(1)=-5$ with $B=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ -5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt gray]{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2})}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)}\n+\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231296 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a74f28-4b3b-5602-b5e5-53c8590d4a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-21887" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+8y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. What Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution $y_p?$" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0437a74-7cb2-5978-b339-e0b17a79e62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372819} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7c971d-59bf-55e6-9232-39b203a6295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372820} {:answer/id #uuid "243bd63f-3232-5656-b4db-8941b50e55f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372821} {:answer/id #uuid "099d26ea-ed99-5004-b3f7-8e7881f83716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243bd63f-3232-5656-b4db-8941b50e55f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372821} :answer-field/id #uuid "126b1668-d791-55ec-9027-d2749d759ab6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f970b89-f8e7-590f-9e3b-8ad7dbd8f9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343669" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{5}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{5}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 4 & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e845a04-8cdb-5e9e-84f7-e0e83c51ac51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455073} {:answer/id #uuid "e5794dc8-ca22-54fc-a518-c6c715487e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{