e $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63827e74-514c-5021-80bc-37896fead1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455084} {:answer/id #uuid "7614d0d6-6711-55f7-ae39-6d253da5157b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455119} {:answer/id #uuid "995b2231-16fd-5d6a-88df-4e7b9bc77275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455182} {:answer/id #uuid "e26b28e3-ff13-5e5c-b422-64465c751cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26b28e3-ff13-5e5c-b422-64465c751cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455293} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47b3730-54e2-5abe-9f7e-75ee2c91ff0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae30a8eb-a3b6-5b76-8879-c28474760fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-344284" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-5,5]$, where $f$ is $10$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $10$-periodic on $[-5,5]$, we have $L=5$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6804d99e-c0f4-5c32-ae56-000926b7e16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372874} {:answer/id #uuid "8e347e67-a037-5fda-affa-f5297b6242db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372875} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7e19e2-75f0-51f6-b02d-6e5432fe5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372876} {:answer/id #uuid "6327d805-8272-54d9-a5e5-49c33f5eaaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7e19e2-75f0-51f6-b02d-6e5432fe5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372876} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2507fa-b3f4-5d1e-bea9-60146188893d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "356f9e42-beaf-5ba9-bd6f-c8cc225eace9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344318" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=3$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 3 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7957bd0d-6987-535b-bc39-c18b21c083c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455125} {:answer/id #uuid "864b690f-5ac5-59c9-93ff-826b478cbf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455144} {:answer/id #uuid "be7d700b-2735-593c-89ba-bbdcc392ae87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455231} {:answer/id #uuid "de07e709-d7da-5f0c-b1a3-0e5395a86a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be7d700b-2735-593c-89ba-bbdcc392ae87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455231} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0817c9-2ceb-5a53-adaf-1b6ad1370f41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de4d297a-9058-5851-b93a-b6b2c81abbee" :question/math-academy-id "q-344324" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+7y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{7}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{7}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 4 & =-A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a266d2-a638-5932-a627-83ee4393036e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454908} {:answer/id #uuid "16ef8407-1674-5a77-bcff-463c5072cce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454943} {:answer/id #uuid "933395f6-d836-5d4f-8cfa-62485055a766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455171} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6bfcaf-0686-58c5-8ea1-d346eaf5ba6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "933395f6-d836-5d4f-8cfa-62485055a766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455171} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99dab0c-9e7c-54ee-9dd8-8fc120d681b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a0cb5bc-257d-52de-82b2-fed9070a3d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-344325" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{5}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{5}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 2 & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fa23ce-2a64-5f21-af68-65dc34a26e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454978} {:answer/id #uuid "966b9893-02ce-5002-92b6-528d0cbf1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455174} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4cbb92-f7b5-5cd0-b586-5f6f713d2615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455258} {:answer/id #uuid "cea66f81-9d52-5528-86a7-49bf7d5e5e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea66f81-9d52-5528-86a7-49bf7d5e5e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0337040-d506-5af7-a704-b40619be2791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a34abd9-b695-5ab7-980c-61ca9112523d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344326" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+10y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+10y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+B\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{10}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{10}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=6$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 6 & =-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}]}
